1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,635
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:09,761
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,761 --> 00:00:10,462
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,521 --> 00:00:13,740
Welcome to Unit 2: Human Body Systems.

5
00:00:14,799 --> 00:00:21,282
When you lift a weight, take a deep breath or feel your heart beating, your body systems are working together.

6
00:00:21,282 --> 00:00:26,890
In this unit, we'll explore the bones and muscles that move us, the lungs that bring in oxygen,

7
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:29,284
and the blood that carries it everywhere.

8
00:00:30,343 --> 00:00:35,775
Our musculoskeletal system is made of bones, joints and muscles. The bones form the skeleton.

9
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:42,265
The skeleton gives the body support and shape, allows it to move, and protects vital organs.

10
00:00:42,265 --> 00:00:46,412
The skull protects the brain, and the ribs protect the heart and lungs.

11
00:00:47,471 --> 00:00:53,195
A newborn baby's bones and muscles are soft and weak, but they grow harder and stronger with age.

12
00:00:53,195 --> 00:00:57,576
Human bone is almost five times as strong as a steel bar of the same mass!

13
00:00:57,576 --> 00:01:00,321
In old age, bones and muscles may weaken again.

14
00:01:01,379 --> 00:01:06,403
Bones can't bend. A joint is a place where two or more bones meet, allowing movement.

15
00:01:06,403 --> 00:01:11,893
Some joints, like those in the skull and the pelvis, don't move at all. They are fixed joints.

16
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:18,909
Moveable joints include ball-and-socket joints. A ball-shaped bone end fits into a cup-shaped socket,

17
00:01:18,909 --> 00:01:22,648
so the bone can move in many directions, even in a full circle.

18
00:01:22,648 --> 00:01:25,510
Your shoulder and hip are ball-and-socket joints.

19
00:01:26,568 --> 00:01:30,540
A hinge joint allows movement back and forth in one direction only,

20
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:35,738
like a door hinge: the door just opens and closes. That comparison is called an analogy.

21
00:01:35,738 --> 00:01:38,834
Your elbow, knee, fingers and toes have hinge joints.

22
00:01:39,892 --> 00:01:44,273
This model arm has two card bones joined by a paper fastener at the elbow,

23
00:01:44,273 --> 00:01:46,493
and a strip of elastic as the biceps.

24
00:01:46,493 --> 00:01:51,808
Predict: will the arm bend if we push the elastic, or only if we pull it? Pause and decide.

25
00:01:52,866 --> 00:01:57,773
Watch. First we push the elastic towards the elbow. It just bends and nothing moves.

26
00:01:58,831 --> 00:02:03,562
Then we pull the elastic, shortening it. The lower arm lifts and the elbow bends.

27
00:02:04,621 --> 00:02:10,462
Explain what you saw. Like the elastic, a muscle can only pull, by contracting and getting shorter.

28
00:02:10,462 --> 00:02:15,660
It cannot push. So muscles work in antagonistic pairs: the biceps pulls to bend the arm,

29
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:20,625
and the triceps pulls to straighten it again. Pause and explain it in your own words.

30
00:02:21,683 --> 00:02:27,583
We have more than 600 muscles. Skeletal muscles are attached to bones by tough cords called tendons.

31
00:02:27,583 --> 00:02:31,964
When a muscle contracts, it gets shorter and fatter and pulls on the bone.

32
00:02:31,964 --> 00:02:37,454
When it relaxes, it returns to its original shape. Muscles can pull, but they can never push!

33
00:02:37,454 --> 00:02:41,952
So they work in pairs that pull in opposite directions: antagonistic muscles.

34
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:46,631
In your arm, the biceps and triceps are an antagonistic pair.

35
00:02:46,631 --> 00:02:52,064
To bend your arm, the biceps contracts and pulls the lower arm up, while the triceps relaxes.

36
00:02:53,122 --> 00:02:59,080
To straighten your arm, the triceps contracts and pulls the lower arm down, while the biceps relaxes.

37
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:03,110
Your thigh muscles work the same way to bend and straighten your leg.
